SDE compositor: window shaders can sample the backdrop of their window.

SDE Liquid Glass (0.2.0 Alpha 2, docs/THEMES.md) draws SDE's panel, popups,
menus and notifications with a window shader that bends, saturates and
lights what is behind them. picom gives window shaders only the window's own
texture; with this patch a window shader (window-shader-fg,
window-shader-fg-rule) that declares

  uniform vec4 backdrop_rect;
  vec4 backdrop_at(vec2 offset);

is linked with a function that returns what is drawn behind the window
`offset` window pixels (x right, y down) from the current fragment: the
windows below it, already blurred when the window has background blur.
backdrop_rect.z is the direction of the window's y axis in gl_FragCoord
(1 or -1) and backdrop_rect.w is 0 when there is no backdrop (the window is
off the screen). Before such a window is drawn, the OpenGL backend copies
the target under it into a texture (one per backend, reused). Damage that
touches such a window grows to cover all of it, because any change under
it changes all of its pixels near the change (the lens of Liquid Glass).

Shaders that do not mention `backdrop` are linked and drawn as before; the
XRender backend has no window shaders. picom's documented window shader
interface (window_shader(), tex, opacity, ...) is unchanged.

